xt  so look at all of the occurrences of all  the words inside them  and get the mean position and then for  um those sections that are sort of  undecided  we look at uh from the middle of that  section and we see  which of the topic means it's closest to  uh and from there we um color it  that color and that's basically  uh how the algorithm works uh and  uh so for um the result if you scroll  down a little bit  uh it's it's unfortunately um  there's like too much data to be uh fit  into like an outlet but there's  um a picture here of what it kind of  looks like  as you can see at the start um it's blue  then brown  blue again and so on this large cross  section you can kind of see  um you know there's little sprinkles of  uh  topics inside other topics and uh that's  that's kind of uh i feel can can have  some sort of value in  understanding how the tech sort of flows  yeah oh sorry now we've gone through  explaining everything let's see how  some examples of how our topic model  actually works so  we've been using alice in wonderland as  an example throughout the uh  the talk so here's a compilation of the  topic progression visualizations for  that text  as you can see some of the words in each  topic are very clearly linked to one  another  such as kings and majesty or queen and  hearts  and queen and hearts are linked together  because of the namesake character who  predominantly shows up in that part of  the text  uh the first topic feeling marked is a  little bit more ambiguous  but it's as you can see from like where  it shows up in the timeline chart  it's obviously about alice entering  wonderline  which wonderland which is why it shows  up in the beginning  um in the frequency start you can see  how some topics show up multiple times  such as the orange near 6000 words or  the brown near the end and that's  something that would only be shown in  the frequency chart and the stack area  plot  not in the timeline chart um the stacked  area plot  shows how all these topics relate to  each other with duration and  frequency etc overall the actual topics  seem to be consistent and representative  of the text  even if some topic titles are slightly  ambiguous  um the example of alice in wonderland  can showcase how our program functions  for fictional texts in general  so we ran the program again for a  peer-reviewed article entitled the  socio-economic implications of the  coronavirus pandemic  which was written by marie nicola at all  we chose this text because we were  curious about how the program would work  for non-fiction informational texts  and um you can see that most of the  topics are very clear-cut  and obviously related such as research  conferences  domestic abuse mortgage house etc there  are also some like japan loan scheme  and oil saudi prices where the topic  tiles are more specific  but you can still see that they're  related to each other in the frequency  chart  you can see blocks of each color which  is a good sign and it makes sense  because  in a research paper especially this one  specifically  topics will be more separated than in a  fictional text  um the stacked area plot is a little bit  different from the alice in wonderland  one because it has more variety in the  curves  you can see where different topics exist  simultaneously whereas for alice in  wonderland  it was a little bit more diagonal  overall this example showcases  how our program can function  successfully for informational texts as  in addition to fictional texts  so in conclusion we successfully  identified and filtered topic clusters  and we created five visualizations for  varying purposes and those would be the  flowchart timeline chart frequency chart  stack area plot and color-coded text  in the future we'd like to allow the  same word to be part of two distinct  topics and we'd also like to  build something that allows for bimodal  topics that occur multiple times  throughout the text  we'd also like to apply our project to  audio clips of conversations and  lectures  um and create a cloud application with  user options to alter the hyper  parameters so that a user could  upload a text and determine their  desired granularity  we'd also like to contribute this to the  wolfram function repository  we would like to thank rory folger the  wolf emerging leaders program  coordinator and william goodall the  assistant coordinator  as well as all the other participants  and the vote for events team for putting  together this conference  thank you so much if you have any  questions you can drop them in the past  full chat  um yeah so i'm gonna upload the notebook  as soon as we finish here  um thank you  if anybody has questions about like our  backgrounds as well or wants to see the  visualizations again we can totally go  through that  oh um rodrigo i'm not sure that i  understand your question  um would you like to unmute yourself  and ask  hi lenny can you hear me uh yes i can  hear you  hi nice first of all nice presentation  and i'd like to know if you you plan to  to do some like a neural net before to  do this team  because like birds you can get paired  the concepts  yes and maybe in instead of a simple  steam  put another layer in the top of that i  don't know if you have training or do  something like that  as an experiment yeah so we definitely  thought about  using neural networks in some way to um  either  come up with the topics or figure out  what hyper parameters work best  um and that's something we totally  consider doing in the future  okay cool  okay so since it doesn't look like there  are any more questions  um well we'd like to thank you for  coming listen to us talk  um we had a lot of fun with this project  and we hope that you enjoyed  our final product um  we can drop our emails in the chat if  any of you would like to um  ask us any more questions  um thank you so much  you
 

